### Problem Description

Right now it is not possible to have reference to `window` of BrowserView in renderer process.

This is however possible for BrowserWindow with `const windowRef = window.open()`. 

In some cases, it'd be very useful to be able to have `window.open()` create new BrowserView in parent window instead of new BrowserWindow.

Example use case:
- we render some external website using BrowserView
- we want to show some content on top of it
- we'd like to allow main renderer process to have DOM control over this overlay part

### Proposed Solution

I'd be like

```tsx
// renderer
const overlayWindow = window.open("about:blank", "overlay");

overlayWindow.document.write("<h1>Hello</h1>");

// main

mainWindow.webContents.setWindowOpenHandler(({ frameName }) => {
  if (frameName === 'overlay') {
    return { action: "allow", useBrowserView: true }
  }
})

mainWindow.on("browser-view-added", (view) => {
  // Manage position, etc here
})
```

### Alternatives Considered

I tried to manage such 'overlay' using new window on top of main window.

I was mirroring their position and size, trying to make it look as if there is only one window.

It was tricky due to multiple reasons: window shadow was flickering when focus was switching from one window to another, same with vibrancy effect, I also had to manage `setIgnoreMouseEvents` with forward options handling a lot of edge-cases

